{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>So shall you do to him as he plotted.</b> And these two latter witnesses who establish the plotting (הזמה) are believed regarding the first witnesses, even if there were a hundred witnesses — and this is astonishing: how do these differ from those, and why should we believe the two latter witnesses more than the first ones, given that the verse decreed to believe two witnesses? But the reason for the matter is that the principals (בעלי דבר) conceded on the basis of the latter ones, for they do not dispute them regarding the essence of the testimony but rather render them wicked through their הזמה by the discrepancy in the places — as though they testified about them that they had profaned the Sabbath or had committed forbidden sexual relations. But if they had directly contradicted their testimony, the testimony of all of them would be withdrawn, like two sets of witnesses who contradict one another. And the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: ״כאשר זמם״ — as he plotted — and not ״כאשר עשה״ — as he did — to say that if the court had already put to death the one liable on the basis of the first witnesses' testimony and afterward they were found to be plotting liars (הוזמו), they are not put to death. And so they said in the Gemara: if they had him put to death, they are not put to death; if they did not have him put to death, they are put to death. And the reason for this is that the verse relied upon the merit of the court, upon whom rests the holy spirit, לא רוח הקודש (רוח הקודש), that they would not spill innocent blood; and had the one put to death not already been liable from another cause, this punishment would not have been brought about through them — therefore they leave the witnesses as though we know that they testified truthfully, and if the one put to death was not liable, the Holy One, blessed be He, has many agents; but when they are found to be plotting liars before any deed was done on their word, they are put to death according to their evil intent." "<b>And one should ask</b> about this, for certainly since the court put him to death on their word, all the more so was he liable, so what difference does it make if he was liable from another side — since they were found to be plotting liars, why are they not put to death? But the view of the Rabbis, of blessed memory, is to say that it is not in our power to put them to death, since the verse does not spell this out." "<b>And in the name of</b> R. Isaiah, of blessed memory, he explains that the reason for 'if they had him put to death, they are not put to death' — that is, if the court had him put to death, they are not put to death — is because we uphold two against two: the two latter witnesses who establish the plotting against the two first ones. 'If they did not have him put to death, they are put to death,' because there are three here: two latter witnesses who establish the plotting, and the principal (בעל דבר) as well, who contradicts the first ones; therefore the two first ones are put to death, for there are three against them. <small>(These words are astonishing and untenable, for even if the plotted-against witnesses were a hundred and the plotters were two, they would still put the plotted-against witnesses to death; and without doubt the words of R. Isaiah, of blessed memory, were garbled in their transcription, and the true version that came from his mouth we do not know. The printer, may his rock protect him, יג״מ.)</small>" "<b>And if</b> you will know why the verse mentioned the two witnesses in singular language, and did not say ״ועשיתם להם כאשר זממו לעשות״ — 'so shall you do to them as they plotted to do' — then you will understand the word ״לאחיו״ — 'to his brother' — that is, as he plotted to do to his elder brother, and he is the Righteous One who cuts down the thick branches of the wicked and uproots the sinning souls, and this is ״כאשר זמם״ — as he plotted — and not ״כאשר עשה״ — as he did — for he did not consent to testify falsely below until he devised false testimony above. And this is the secret that the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'if they had him put to death, they are not put to death,' because the elder brother has already taken his portion, and this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:44.608783705Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}